From: serlk <22211586+Serlk@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] packages: a catalogue keyed by package name, verified installs, permissions, component access

An application used to be whatever APK file was started: its data directory
was named after the file, "installing" copied the file under its own name
and started the application to learn its package name, and other packages
were looked up by a different file name than the one they were stored
under. This puts an installed-package catalogue underneath
(android.atl.ATLPackageStore, <data dir>/_packages_/<package>/):

- The identity of an installation is the package name of the verified
  manifest. Data, libraries, application id, launcher entry and D-Bus
  activation file all hang off it; the name of the source file plays no
  role, so an update from a renamed file keeps the data and two files
  called base.apk install two packages.
- Installing runs nothing of the package. Every file's signature is
  verified over the whole file (JAR signature, APK Signature Scheme v2 and
  v3 with proof-of-rotation, written from the published format
  descriptions: ATLApkVerifier), then package name, version, signer set,
  split set (one base, distinct splits, isSplitRequired, requiredSplitTypes,
  configForSplit) and native code (a package with libraries but none for
  this machine is refused with the ABIs it has) are checked. An update needs
  the same signers or a proven succession from the installed one, and a
  version that is not older unless asked for.
- A version is staged next to its destination, synced, and published by
  renaming the directory and then the "current" link: an interrupted or
  refused installation leaves the previous version and its data untouched.
  Superseded versions go when no process holds them.
- The launcher entry and the D-Bus service file are written from the
  published version and removed with it; the entry follows the enabled
  launcher activity or alias.
- `android-translation-layer --pm ...` is the command line of the
  catalogue (install, uninstall, list, info, path, components,
  enable/disable/default, permissions, grant/revoke, migrate-data); it runs
  in a VM without an application and needs no display. --install and
  --install-internal map onto it. `--package NAME` starts an installed
  package under its own application id, one process per package (a second
  start is handed to the running one); `--background` starts it without a
  window for a request from another package and ends when no service runs.
- Data of the file-name based layout is taken over only when its owner is
  confirmed (the APK of that name has the package name and a signer of the
  installed version) and only if there is exactly one such directory;
  otherwise nothing is moved until the user names one.

On top of the catalogue:

- PackageManager: installed packages and applications, uid and signature
  queries, installer name, install times; component enabled states are
  stored per package, obeyed by resolution and survive updates;
  getSystemAvailableFeatures() and hasSystemFeature() answer from one list.
- PackageInstaller sessions and the install/uninstall intents end in the
  same catalogue operation after the user confirmed it, and report the
  status to the caller's IntentSender or activity result.
- PendingIntent is a real token (identity, update/cancel/one-shot/immutable
  flags, getIntentSender, send with fill-in); AlarmManager can cancel.
- Permissions: normal ones follow the manifest, signature ones the real
  signer, the ones that guard something of the user's need the user's
  decision (ATLPermissions). Activity.requestPermissions asks and delivers
  onRequestPermissionsResult; decisions are stored with the package and can
  be changed from outside while the application runs.
- Services have start/bind/unbind lifetimes; another package's components
  are not run inside this process any more (except the Play services
  family, as before), and requests that arrive over D-Bus only reach
  enabled, exported components without a permission. The package's own
  notification actions carry a per-package key instead.
- The signature that clients expect of a Play services provider is only
  reported for a package really signed by a certificate listed in
  /etc/atl/gms-signers; updates and signature comparisons use the real
  signer. The built-in stand-in for Play services is off unless ATL_GSTUB=1.
- AccountManager: an account is visible to the authenticator of its type
  and to packages it was made visible to, instead of to everyone; the
  key store no longer logs the password argument.
- Notifications: POST_NOTIFICATIONS and channel importance are obeyed, tag
  and id together name a notification, channels are kept.

